Well... don't "charge" the lower levels.  But don't waste a lot of time on
them either.

The way I do it is I get set up a lot like you -- get my character pretty
buffed up before I go below the castle, make sure I have AC -45 or better
(seriously! This makes Gehennom a lot easier) and at least 2 blessed
unihorns in my inventory (and one in my bag) as well as a couple other
methods of healing / sickness curing.  I also make sure I have enough holy
water and enough ways to detect objects that I can clear at least the
first 6-8 levels of Gehennom easily (blessed potions are nice for this,
and Wizards have enough spells to clear each Gehennom level easily.)  Then
I drop down a level, clear out the valley, and generally just push my way
downward, not spending way too long on any one level.

I do one thing I haven't heard others mention before, though -- once I
have all the levels in Gehennom mapped out, I come back up, and not only
dig a fairly wide path between stairs, but I dig a few other gaps in the
level so it'll be easy to cross if I get pulled back and end up somewhere
random.  I dig perhaps the center, top, and bottom rows, and about 5-6
columns spread out across the map.  This way, wherever I end up, it'll
only take me about 10 turns to get onto one of these "direct" paths across
the level, and then I can run quickly toward the stairs.



